{"name":"Liquid Mercury","hex":"#757a80","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liquid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A substance that is flowing, and keeping no shape, such as water; a substance of which the molecules, while not tending to separate from one another like those of a gas, readily change their relative position, and which therefore retains no definite shape, except that determined by the containing receptacle; an inelastic fluid.","Any of a class of consonant sounds that includes l and r."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Flowing freely like water; fluid; not solid and not gaseous; composed of particles that move freely among each other on the slightest pressure.","Easily sold or disposed of without losing value."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liquid"},{"term":"mercury","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Senses relating to the metal.\n A silvery-coloured, metallic chemical element with the atomic number 80; it is liquid at room temperature, and toxic. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A silvery-coloured, metallic chemical element with the atomic number 80; it is liquid at room temperature, and toxic. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply or coat (something) with mercury (noun sense 1) or a preparation containing mercury.\n\n, to apply to (the face) a preparation of mercury to beautify it",", to apply to (the face) a preparation of mercury to beautify it"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mercury"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mercury (element)","blocks":[{"text":"Mercury is a chemical element; it has symbol Hg and atomic number 80. It is commonly known as quicksilver. A heavy, silvery d-block element, mercury is the only metallic element that is known to be liquid at standard temperature and pressure. As well as having the lowest freezing point, mercury has the lowest boiling point and subsequently the narrowest liquid state range of any metal at standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Pouring_liquid_mercury_bionerd.jpg/330px-Pouring_liquid_mercury_bionerd.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury_(element)","wikidata":"Q925"},{"title":"Liquid","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid is a state of matter with a definite volume but no fixed shape. When confined in a container and subject to a force such as gravity, liquids will adapt to the internal shape of the container in the direction of the force. Liquids are nearly incompressible, maintaining their volume even under pressure. The density of a liquid is usually close to that of a solid, and much higher than that of a gas. Liquids are a form of condensed matter alongside solids, and a form of fluid alongside gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/330px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid","wikidata":"Q11435"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/500px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/1280px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg/500px-Pound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A round pound coin (density ~7.6 g/cm3) floats on mercury due to the buoyancy force upon it and appears to float higher because of the strong surface tension of the mercury.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APound-coin-floating-in-mercury.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Mercury-27128.jpg/500px-Mercury-27128.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Mercury-27128.jpg/500px-Mercury-27128.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Native mercury with cinnabar, Socrates mine, Sonoma County, California. Cinnabar sometimes alters to native mercury in the oxidized zone of mercury deposits.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMercury-27128.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/HgProductionPrice.png/500px-HgProductionPrice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/HgProductionPrice.png/1280px-HgProductionPrice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/HgProductionPrice.png/500px-HgProductionPrice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Evolution of mercury price (U.S.) and production (worldwide)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHgProductionPrice.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Amalgam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/Amalgam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Dental amalgam filling a decayed molar tooth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmalgam.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/2023_Rt%C4%99ciowy_termometr_lekarski.jpg/250px-2023_Rt%C4%99ciowy_termometr_lekarski.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb